  "versionNotes": [
    {
      "edition": "ASP.NET Core 11 / .NET 11 GA checked 2026-11-20",
      "items": [
        "Starting in ASP.NET Core 11, built-in HTTP server meters and request activities carry the required OpenTelemetry HTTP semantic-convention data. The hosting Activity source adds HTTP attributes by default, resolves conventional-route parameters in http.route, adds error.type to 5xx request-duration measurements, and records unhandled exceptions as standard Activity events without setting StatusDescription. The OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.AspNetCore package is optional for these built-in HTTP signals; teach direct MeterProviderBuilder.AddMeter and TracerProviderBuilder.AddSource subscription, and explain when the package remains useful for its additional instrumentation features.",
        "ASP.NET Core 10 added built-in authentication and authorization metrics, and ASP.NET Core 10 added built-in Blazor component metrics. Present these as available in .NET 10 and later rather than attributing them to .NET 11.",
        "ASP.NET Core 7 added the Grpc.AspNetCore.HealthChecks package integration for exposing .NET health checks through the standard gRPC health protocol; distinguish it from the built-in HTTP health middleware.",
        "Use the current System.Diagnostics.Metrics and Activity APIs for new .NET instrumentation. EventCounters and DiagnosticSource remain relevant for existing tools and legacy instrumentations, but are not equivalent replacements for new metrics and ActivitySource traces.",
        "The ASP.NET Core health-check abstractions are framework-provided. Third-party checks such as AspNetCore.Diagnostics.HealthChecks are separate community packages; don't imply that their database or UI registrations are built into ASP.NET Core.",
        "The .NET health-check telemetry publisher is opt-in via AddTelemetryHealthCheckPublisher and exposes dotnet.health_check.reports and dotnet.health_check.unhealthy_checks for push-based metrics only; these metrics are unavailable to pull-based collection. The extension is provided by Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks.Common for supported target versions.",
        "Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks.Common also provides manual health-check control and an application-lifecycle health provider; Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks.ResourceUtilization provides an optional CPU/memory health check. Keep these package-provided extensions distinct from the ASP.NET Core HTTP endpoint middleware.",
        "The current ResourceUtilizationHealthCheckOptions exposes CPU/memory thresholds and UseObservableResourceMonitoringInstruments; SamplingWindow is obsolete. CPU and memory thresholds default to null. The observable-instrument option is experimental and defaults to false, so the health check otherwise uses the deprecated IResourceMonitor path. Resource monitoring itself is transitioning toward observable metrics; check package-specific APIs and platform behavior."
      ],
